\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Impurity-only enrichment for the ground state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Partition the single-particle space into
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 (impurity),
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 (active bath),
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 (Slater), with sizes
\begin_inset Formula $n_{I},n_{A},n_{S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Blocks and state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The one-body matrix in block form:
\begin_inset Formula \[
K=\begin{pmatrix} K_{II} & K_{IA} & K_{IS}\\ K_{AI} & K_{AA} & K_{AS}\\ K_{SI} & K_{SA} & K_{SS}\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B\equiv\begin{pmatrix}K_{AA}&K_{AS}\\ K_{SA}&K_{SS}\end{pmatrix}\ \text{(bath one-body)}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Write
\begin_inset Formula $H=H_{\mathrm{imp}}+V+H_{\mathrm{bath}}$
\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula $H_{\mathrm{imp}}$
\end_inset

 the impurity (
\begin_inset Formula $K_{II}$
\end_inset

 plus interaction
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

),
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 the impurity-bath hopping (
\begin_inset Formula $K_{IA},K_{IS}$
\end_inset

 and h.c.), and
\begin_inset Formula $H_{\mathrm{bath}}=\sum_{ab}B_{ab}c_{a}^{\dagger}c_{b}$
\end_inset

 over
\begin_inset Formula $a,b\in A\cup S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
State ansatz: an entangled block on
\begin_inset Formula $I\cup A$
\end_inset

 times a Slater determinant on
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula \[
|\Psi\rangle=|\mathrm{MPS}\rangle_{I\cup A}\otimes|\Phi\rangle_{S},\qquad
|\Phi\rangle_{S}=\prod_{\mu=1}^{N_{S}}\Big(\sum_{i\in S}\Phi_{i\mu}\,c_{i}^{\dagger}\Big)|0\rangle,\qquad\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi=\mathbb{1}_{N_{S}},
\]

\end_inset

so
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

 is the
\begin_inset Formula $n_{S}\times N_{S}$
\end_inset

 matrix of occupied orbitals, columns orthonormal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The split-step
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Trotter the imaginary-time step,
\begin_inset Formula \[
e^{-H\,d\tau}\;\approx\;\underbrace{e^{-H_{\mathrm{bath}}\,d\tau}}_{\text{single-particle}}\;\cdot\;\underbrace{e^{-(H_{\mathrm{imp}}+\tilde{V})\,d\tau}}_{\text{MPS step}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second factor is taken in the interaction picture w.r.t.

\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, so
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is
\series bold
removed
\series default
 from the generator and
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is replaced by its first Magnus-corrected form
\begin_inset Formula \[
\tilde{V}=V-\tfrac{d\tau}{2}\,[\,B,V\,]+\mathcal{O}(d\tau^{2}),
\]

\end_inset

whose impurity
\begin_inset Formula $\to$
\end_inset

bath block is, in matrix terms,
\begin_inset Formula $K_{I,\mathrm{bath}}\big(\mathbb{1}-\tfrac{d\tau}{2}B\big)$
\end_inset

.
 This is the imaginary-time analog of the commutator term in the dynamics (there it carried a
\begin_inset Formula $-i\,d\tau/2$
\end_inset

; here it is real,
\begin_inset Formula $-d\tau/2$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Why enrichment becomes impurity-only
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Enrichment looks at the coupling from the MPS window
\begin_inset Formula $I\cup A$
\end_inset

 to the Slater orbitals
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, i.e.
 the SVD of
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{pmatrix}K_{IS}\\ K_{AS}\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset

Removing
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 from the MPS generator sets the active-bath row to zero,
\begin_inset Formula $K_{AS}\to0$
\end_inset

, leaving only
\begin_inset Formula $K_{IS}$
\end_inset

.
 So
\begin_inset Formula \[
\operatorname{rank}\!\begin{pmatrix}K_{IS}\\ K_{AS}\end{pmatrix}=n_{\mathrm{ch}}+\operatorname{rank}(K_{AS})\;\longrightarrow\;\operatorname{rank}(K_{IS})=n_{\mathrm{ch}}.
\]

\end_inset

That
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{rank}(K_{AS})$
\end_inset

 is exactly the surplus you pay now and the part that is
\emph on
invariant under any rotation of
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset


\emph default
 -- which is why rotating into the bath eigenbasis could never remove it; you have to drop the block, not rotate it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The free-bath step and the QR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first factor,
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-H_{\mathrm{bath}}d\tau}$
\end_inset

, acts on the Slater.
 Restricted to
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 the generator is
\begin_inset Formula $K_{SS}$
\end_inset

, and the single-particle propagator is
\begin_inset Formula \[
g=e^{-K_{SS}\,d\tau}\qquad(n_{S}\times n_{S},\ \text{symmetric positive, non-unitary}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By Thouless' theorem a one-body exponential maps a Slater determinant to a Slater determinant, with the occupied orbitals transported as
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\to\Phi'=g\,\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 The problem:
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi'$
\end_inset

 is
\series bold
no longer orthonormal
\series default
,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\Phi'^{\dagger}\Phi'=\Phi^{\dagger}g^{\dagger}g\,\Phi=\Phi^{\dagger}e^{-2K_{SS}d\tau}\Phi\neq\mathbb{1}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A determinant is invariant, as a physical ray, under right-multiplication of its orbital matrix by any invertible
\begin_inset Formula $N_{S}\times N_{S}$
\end_inset

 matrix
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

, changing only the scalar prefactor by
\begin_inset Formula $\det M$
\end_inset

.
 So pick
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 to re-orthonormalize via a thin QR:
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{\;\Phi'=Q\,R,\qquad Q^{\dagger}Q=\mathbb{1}_{N_{S}},\quad R\ \text{upper-triangular}.\;}
\]

\end_inset

Then the canonical (orthonormal-orbital) representative of the
\emph on
same
\emph default
 state is
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\Phi'R^{-1}$
\end_inset

, and the weight is carried by
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula \[
|\Phi'\rangle=\det(R)\,|Q\rangle,\qquad\langle\Phi'|\Phi'\rangle=|\det R|^{2}.
\]

\end_inset

So
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 feeds back as the new Slater orbitals, and
\begin_inset Formula $\det R$
\end_inset

 is the imaginary-time weight folded into the energy/normalization.
 Because
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is a contraction,
\begin_inset Formula $|\det R|<1$
\end_inset

 and the occupied modes drift toward the bottom of the bath spectrum -- i.e.
 occupations driven to
\begin_inset Formula $0/1$
\end_inset

, which is exactly what makes those orbitals demote cleanly out of the active region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Why this QR is invisible in real time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the dynamics the same step has
\begin_inset Formula $g=e^{-iK_{SS}dt}$
\end_inset

,
\series bold
unitary
\series default
.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula \[
\Phi'^{\dagger}\Phi'=\Phi^{\dagger}g^{\dagger}g\,\Phi=\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi=\mathbb{1},
\]

\end_inset

so
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi'$
\end_inset

 is already orthonormal: the QR returns
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\Phi'$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $R=$
\end_inset

 diagonal phases.
 No re-orthonormalization,
\begin_inset Formula $|\det R|=1$
\end_inset

 -- the orbitals merely pick up phases.
 In imaginary time the phase becomes a real damping,
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 becomes nontrivially triangular, and the QR is the price of non-unitarity: one
\begin_inset Formula $n_{S}\times N_{S}$
\end_inset

 factorization per step, entirely at the single-particle level, never touching the MPS bond dimension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the bath eigenbasis
\begin_inset Formula $K_{SS}=\operatorname{diag}(\varepsilon)$
\end_inset

 the whole thing is diagonal:
\begin_inset Formula $g=\operatorname{diag}(e^{-\varepsilon d\tau})$
\end_inset

, and if
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

 already occupies the lowest modes,
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi'=g\Phi$
\end_inset

 is orthonormal up to per-column scalars --
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is diagonal and the QR collapses to a rescaling.
 That is the cheapest realization of the idea, and it is exactly where ``rotate to the bath eigenbasis in between'' pays off: not to kill
\begin_inset Formula $K_{AS}$
\end_inset

 (impossible by rotation), but to make this free-bath step trivially diagonal.
\end_layout

